WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.850
So how do you get from a blip to a decision?

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.625
You plot it.

3
00:00:04.875 --> 00:00:09.000
Mark the target’s position several times, over a few minutes.

4
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.125
The line through those marks is its relative motion line.

5
00:00:13.375 --> 00:00:16.725
That’s how the target is moving in relation to you.

6
00:00:16.975 --> 00:00:20.050
Extend that line past the center of the screen.

7
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.650
The closest it comes to the center is the closest point of approach, the C P A.

8
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.725
The time until then is the T C P A.

9
00:00:28.975 --> 00:00:33.075
So if the line runs straight through the center, what’s your C P A?

10
00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:35.475
Zero.

11
00:00:35.725 --> 00:00:37.700
That’s a collision course.

12
00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.800
Many radars will track targets for you.

13
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.625
On small boats it’s called MARPA, on ships ARPA.

14
00:00:44.875 --> 00:00:47.825
And Rule seven is behind all of this.

15
00:00:48.075 --> 00:00:52.625
Plotting, or equivalent systematic observation, is required.

16
00:00:52.875 --> 00:00:56.275
No decisions on scanty radar information.
